A||||8||pontiacs4me||3.4 swap, computer/wiring questions.||||1||
Z||000000||pontiacs4me||05-28-2001||03:39 AM||||I was wanting to do a 3800 swap, but ive been reading up on the 3.4 swap and it sounds kind of cool.  A few questions on the wiring though.  Do the sensors interchange between the 2.8 and the 3.4?  What about the computer system.  Should I just get the computer and wiring from the 3.4?  How does the wiring harness hook into the rest of the car (console hookups).  What years of 3.4 are most desirable?  What can be done to make about 240 horse out of one of these without spending 10000000 dollars?  thanks for any info.||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||05-28-2001||09:59 AM||||Which 3.4L? Iron head or DOHC?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Steve Normington||05-28-2001||11:20 AM||||Bespeaks one who has learned the hard way:<P>The aluminum head Generation III 3.4 engines will not fit without a lot of work. The intake is different, the exhaust is different, the timing cover/water pump is different, the alternator/compressor bolt patterns are different, the distributor will not fit, and the motor mount is different.<P>If you go with the 93-95 Camaro/Firebird 3.4, the only thing you'll need to do is drill and tap 2 holes. You'll be able to use your exisiting Fiero parts.<P>As far as the horsepower goes, you're going to have to get pretty radical to get 240 HP (I'm assuming NA and no NOS).<P>Porting the exhaust<BR>Gasket matching the intake<BR>1.6 roller rockers<BR>Roller lifters<BR>Performance cam<BR>Bore out the cylinders<BR>High volume oil pump<P>Probably your best bet is to get the strongest valve train componants, with the high volume pump, this should let you hit a higher red-line.<P>------------------<BR>I don't car if my next Fiero is a 2M Hamster, I'm not swapping the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000003||BV MotorSports||05-28-2001||03:09 PM||||If I were you I would cease all thoughts of a 3.4 swap. No really. The 3.8 has 205 (K code series II) and 225-235 ft-lbs of torque.<BR>3.4 is rated at 160-170hp and 190-205ft-lbs.<P>The 3.8 is the best engine swap for the fiero bar none. I dont know why GM didnt just put in in the Fiero in the first place.<BR>A bone stock 3.8 fiero would run low 14's!<BR>Then there is always the 3.8sc thats a low 13 sec stock fiero!<P>It kinda breaks down (and lets be honest) to how much $$$ you have to spend. If you cant afford the $$ for a 3.8 go with the 3.4 but...you would have to mod the heck out of it to make the same HP as the 3.8n/a bone stock. 3.4's are getting harder to find w/ low miles and the 3.8 is called the small block chevy of the 90's. They are in just about every FWD GM makes (mid size-up).<P>I have driven a few 3.8n/a and sc cars and that s/c is a freggen beast! Im mean wow!<BR>The n/a is a very stout engine and you know it when you put your foot down. These engines are VERY reliable. Plan to spend approx 1k more for the s/c motor. You can buy the n/a for about the same as a 3.4 and may run into problems finding a shop that will drill the holes on the other side of the block for the starter. Some claim to have drilled them @ home but if you mess up at all....I guess you got a nice paper wieght or boat anchor.<P>Steven<P>------------------<BR>87 Fiero GT 14.9/ 93mph<BR>94 Formula WS6 w/ NOS 12.4 /118mph<BR>87 Conquest TSi <BR>85.5 Starion ESi||||reg||4
Z||000004||Brucepts||05-28-2001||05:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BV MotorSports:<BR><B>If I were you I would cease all thoughts of a 3.4 swap. No really. The 3.8 has 205 (K code series II) and 225-235 ft-lbs of torque.<BR>3.4 is rated at 160-170hp and 190-205ft-lbs.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>At what rpm's? HP and torque numbers without rpm really don't mean a whole lot if you are comparing. If you are trying to figureout a motor swap for a street motor, I want to go with the one that has the best torque curve not the most HP in some rpm range the motor will see but a few times. Which one has the most low end "grunt" for the $$$'s in a streetable rpm range?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Advanced Dealer in the Great Material Continuum . . .<BR>Non Fiero Website: <A HREF="http://tractorsport.com" TARGET=_blank>http://tractorsport.com</A> <BR>You can't race a dyno . . .||||reg||1
Z||000005||Shaun41178||05-28-2001||07:07 PM||||use the search function.  this has been covered to death to death to death.  You should find all the info and then some<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||BV MotorSports||05-28-2001||07:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Brucepts:<BR><B> At what rpm's? HP and torque numbers without rpm really don't mean a whole lot if you are comparing. If you are trying to figureout a motor swap for a street motor, I want to go with the one that has the best torque curve not the most HP in some rpm range the motor will see but a few times. Which one has the most low end "grunt" for the $$$'s in a streetable rpm range?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Then the 3.8 is the clear winner any way you look at it even in n/a form.<P>Think of it this way:<P>2.8 has 140hp and 170ftlbs<BR>3.4 has 160hp and 190ftlbs<BR>3.8n/a has 205hp and 235ftlbs<BR>3.8s/c has 240hp and 270ftlbs.<P>3.4 offers 20hp increase in hp ands 20ftlbs<BR>3.8n/a offers 65hp and 65ftlbs! <BR>3.8s/c offers 100hp and 100ftlbs.<P>Hmmmm not a hard choice to make there. Like I said, it all comes down to how much $$$ you are willing to spend. The 3.8's are light yrs ahead of the 60deg v6. And there are way more options for future mods.<P>Steven<BR><P>------------------<BR>87 Fiero GT 14.9/ 93mph<BR>94 Formula WS6 w/ NOS 12.4 /118mph<BR>87 Conquest TSi <BR>85.5 Starion ESi||||reg||1
Z||000007||88formula||05-28-2001||08:06 PM||||The 3.4-liter engine has 160 horsepower and 200-ft. lbs. of torque. The Series two 3800 engine varies but most have 205 horsepower and 225-ft. lbs. of torque. The first generation 3800 engine has 165 horsepower and 210-ft. lbs. of torque. The SC 3800 engine has 240 horsepower and 280-ft. lbs. of torque. <P>The 3.8-liter engine is as you said a better engine but if the owner wants to keep the stock look or doesn't want to spend lots of money and make all kinds of custom parts than the 3.4-liter engine is hard to beat. I think the 3.4-liter engine is lighter too.<P>If all you care about is TORQUE and which motor will be the fastest than nothing beats the small block Chevy especially if you consider cost and ease of modification.<P>I wonder how a 3.4-liter engine with a turbocharger on it would fare against the SC 3800 engine? <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||BV MotorSports||05-28-2001||08:42 PM||||3.4 lighter? How so? <BR>Have to make custom parts? <BR>Everything you need to perform the swap is offered thru 3 different companies. 3.8 swap is "stupid easy"!<P>Once swapped in it looks factory. May not have that high restriction intake of the 2.8 but....I can live with out it.<P>Dennis L has a 3.4 based turbo engine and its a very nice example of a low cost turbo set up when he first started it (pre 3.4).<BR>I think his first attempt ended up with 200hp with the 2.8 .<P>JR has a 3.8s/c in a Countach kit he built and it was measured @ 4.2 zero to 60. And thats in a 100-200# HEAVIER kit car!<P>Once again it all boils down to how much $$ you have to spend.<P>Oh, and yes....3.8 hp figures are based on the series II K code like I stated eariler.<BR>I wouldnt even consider series I engines.<P>Steven<P>------------------<BR>87 Fiero GT 14.9/ 93mph<BR>94 Formula WS6 w/ NOS 12.4 /118mph<BR>87 Conquest TSi <BR>85.5 Starion ESi||||reg||1
